{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Understanding Differences Between C and C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 12}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear/use when comparing programming languages."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe a general-purpose computer programming language, regarding its history and architecture in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect Versus Past Simple"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The present perfect is used to talk about unfinished actions that started in the past.  The past simple is used to talk about actions that finished in the past."}, {"type": "example", "content": "We have already said that both C and C# are versatile and widely used programming languages."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Engineers have used this language for tasks ranging from system programming app to even chip designing or programming for embedded devices."}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use the <strong>present perfect</strong> to talk about actions that started in the past and still occur or have connection with the present. We use the <strong>past simple</strong> for actions that started and finished in the past. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We also use the <strong>present perfect</strong> to talk about finished actions in the past that have a result in the present."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present perfect:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Interrogative form:<br>  <strong>Have/has</strong> + subject + <strong>verb past participle</strong> + complement. </br>Example: <i><strong>Have</strong> you <strong>used</strong> C# lately?</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Affirmative form:<br> Subject  + <strong>have/has</strong> + <strong>verb past participle</strong> + complement. </br>Example: <i>I <strong>have started</strong> a new project with C#.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Negative form:</br> Subject + <strong>have/has</strong> + not + <strong>verb past participle</strong> + complement. </br>Example: <i>I <strong>have not started</strong> the new project yet!</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Simple past:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Interrogative form: <br><strong>Did</strong> + subject + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br></br>Example: <i><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>use</strong> C# in your last project?</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Affirmative form:<br> Subject + <strong>verb past simple</strong> + complement. </br></br>Example: <i>I <strong>started</strong> a new project with C#.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Negative form:</br> Subject + <strong>did</strong> + not + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement. </br></br>Example: <i>I <strong>did not start</strong> the project yesterday!</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In the programming industry, major companies have preferred to hire people who have the ability to read and use both C and C#. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Future With Will and Going To"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use <strong>will</strong> and <strong>going to</strong> to talk about future. However, there are some slight differences that make you choose one or the other."}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use the future simple with <strong>will</strong> for:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "\u2022\tPredictions without evidence"}, {"type": "text", "content": "\u2022\tDecisions made at the moment of speaking "}, {"type": "text", "content": " \u2022\tPromises or requests"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Affirmative form:<br> Subject <strong>will</strong> + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i>I <strong>will</strong> start a new project with C#.</i></br></br>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Negative form:</br> Subject + <strong>will</strong> + not + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i>I <strong>will not use</strong> C++ for this project.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Interrogative form: <br><strong>Will</strong> + subject + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i><strong>Will</strong> you <strong>use</strong> C# in your next project?</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use the future with <strong>going to</strong> for:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "\u2022\tSomething you had decided to do before"}, {"type": "text", "content": "\u2022\tA prediction made on evidence (that we see or hear)"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Affirmative form:<br> Subject + verb to be <strong>going to</strong> + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i>I <strong>am going to use</strong> C++ for all my projects this year.</i></br></br>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Negative form:</br> Subject + + verb to be + not + <strong>going to</strong> + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i>I am not<strong> going to program</strong> this task today; I'll do it tomorrow.</i>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Interrogative form: <br> Verb to be + subject + <strong>going to</strong> + <strong>verb base form</strong> + complement.</br>Example: <i>Are you <strong>going to use</strong> C# in your next project?</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In today's session, we are going to see some characteristics of both of them, and at a point, we will compare them."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Overall, both languages are going to be essential to meet your programming needs. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "We will start by saying that C is a structure-oriented language while C# is an object-oriented one."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "standardized "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "adopted as a standard "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It came as a structured and standardized programming language mainly used for scientific and management purposes. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "function "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a code with a specific property "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: C organizes files and modules; each file has some specific features like data, processes, and functions. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "hybrid "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something made by combining two different elements "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In 1999, a man named Anders Hejlsberg developed C#. This language is technically a hybrid between C and C++ and is suitable for a wide variety of purposes due to its flexibility. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "flexibility "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "versatility "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In 1999, a man named Anders Hejlsberg developed C#. This language is technically a hybrid between C and C++ and is suitable for a huge variety of purposes due to its flexibility. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "range"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to go from one to another limit"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Engineers have used this language for tasks ranging from system programming app to even chip designing or programming for embedded devices."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob12aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Review the information gathered from this object. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Complete a Venn diagram.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "In the left and right section, write differences of each C. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "In the middle, write similarities of C, C++ and C#. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Discuss your Venn diagram with a partner."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I review the information in this object. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I complete a Venn diagram. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discuss the Venn diagram with a partner."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob12re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "We have already said that both C and C# are versatile and widely used programming languages. In today's session, we are going to see some characteristics of both of them, and at a point, we will compare them. In any case, we will not say that one is better than the other; they were designed for different purposes. In 1969, a man named Dennis Ritchie designed C language. It came as a structured and standardized programming language mainly used for scientific and management purposes. C organizes files and modules; each file has some specific features like data, processes, and functions. Engineers have used this language for tasks ranging from system programming app to even chip designing or programming for embedded devices."}, {"time": "47000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob12re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "In 1999, a man named Anders Hejlsberg developed C#. This language is technically a hybrid between C and C++ and is suitable for a wide variety of purposes due to its flexibility. One of its most significant uses is for Windows application development. C# is now almost every developer's first choice for building Windows desktop applications thanks to being user-friendly. It is also widely used for the development of mobile applications, which is one of the fastest growing markets in the programming world. C# can also be used for programming and coding web applications, making it extremely useful for website designers and advertisers alike. When comparing these two languages, we will start by saying that C is a structure-oriented language while C# is an object-oriented one."}, {"time": "106000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob12re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Overall, both languages are going to be essential to meet your programming needs. In the programming industry, major companies have preferred to hire people who have the ability to read and use both C and C#. In the future, it is very likely that the way we program will change completely, but these days, C and C# have been and are going to remain two of the most important programming languages."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob12re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob12re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n5le03ob12re5vsc01\t\t\t", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""